<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  autounattend.xml TEMPLATE — rendered by guest/build.py, never used as-is.

  Placeholders are double-at delimited; the renderer refuses to emit a file with
  any of them left. The rendered file carries an account name and a plaintext
  password, so it is machine-local and never enters a repository; only this
  template does. Rendered output lives 0700 under ~/.images, machine-local.

  Prose here uses em dashes because a literal double hyphen is illegal inside an
  XML comment; the renderer parses what it produces, so the mistake cannot ship.

  WHY A SEPARATE HELPER ISO. Setup looks for autounattend.xml in the root of
  every attached drive, so the 8.5 GB Windows ISO is never repacked — a tiny
  ISO with this file at its root is enough, and it is cheap to rebuild between
  attempts. That is the whole reason this file can be iterated on at all.

  MEASURED, NOT GUESSED (2026-08-05, tr-TR 25H2 ISO):

    * The ISO has NO sources/EI.cfg, so Setup would show the edition picker.
      <ImageInstall> answers it.
    * install.wim holds SIX images and no N editions:
        1 Home  2 Home Single Language  3 Education
        4 Pro   5 Pro Education         6 Pro for Workstations
      The widely repeated "consumer editions" order has eleven images with Pro
      at index 6 — selecting by INDEX would have installed Pro for
      Workstations here. The image NAME is in English even on the tr-TR media,
      so /IMAGE/NAME is both correct and stable across ISO revisions.
    * The guest passes the Windows 11 hardware checks on its own (TPM 2.0 via
      tpm-crb, Secure Boot capable firmware, 8 vCPU, 12 GB), which is why there
      is no LabConfig/bypass block here. The manual install in an earlier
      session proved it.

  THE viostor FRICTION DIES IN THE windowsPE PASS. The virtio disk is invisible
  to Setup until viostor is injected. Drive letters are unstable, so every
  plausible letter is listed; a path that does not exist is ignored. NetKVM
  rides along for the same price — drivers injected here also land in the
  installed image, so the guest has network without a second pass.

  THE KEYSTONE IS qemu-ga, NOT THE INSTALL. Everything after this file happens
  over guest-exec, which does not exist until the agent is running. So the
  agent install is the one thing this file must not get wrong. It is deliberately
  NOT an msiexec call in the specialize pass: Windows Installer is not reliably
  available that early. Instead specialize copies setupcomplete.cmd off the
  helper ISO into C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\, and Setup runs it as SYSTEM in a
  fully-serviced OS right before the first logon.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

  <settings pass="windowsPE">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <SetupUILanguage>
        <UILanguage>@@UILANG@@</UILanguage>
      </SetupUILanguage>
      <InputLocale>@@INPUTLOCALE@@</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>@@SYSLOCALE@@</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>@@UILANG@@</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>@@USERLOCALE@@</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <!-- THREE DRIVERS, THREE DISTINCT REASONS. None is decoration:

           viostor   the boot disk is virtio-blk and Setup cannot see it at all
                     without this. Miss it and there is no disk to install to.
           NetKVM    the guest's network.
           vioserial the transport qemu-ga talks over. MEASURED THE HARD WAY on
                     2026-08-05: the first round injected only the first two,
                     the agent installed cleanly and its service was RUNNING,
                     and the host still got "QEMU guest agent is not connected"
                     — a running agent with no channel looks exactly like an
                     agent that was never installed. The DriverStore had
                     viostor and netkvm and no vioser.

         Drive letters are unstable; list them all. Nonexistent paths are
         skipped, so the cost of an extra letter is nothing. -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-PnpCustomizationsWinPE"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <DriverPaths>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="1"><Path>D:\viostor\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="2"><Path>E:\viostor\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="3"><Path>F:\viostor\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="4"><Path>G:\viostor\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="5"><Path>H:\viostor\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="6"><Path>D:\NetKVM\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="7"><Path>E:\NetKVM\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="8"><Path>F:\NetKVM\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="9"><Path>G:\NetKVM\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="10"><Path>H:\NetKVM\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="11"><Path>D:\vioserial\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="12"><Path>E:\vioserial\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="13"><Path>F:\vioserial\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="14"><Path>G:\vioserial\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
        <PathAndCredentials wcm:action="add" wcm:keyValue="15"><Path>H:\vioserial\w11\amd64</Path></PathAndCredentials>
      </DriverPaths>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">

      <!-- GPT/UEFI: ESP + MSR + one OS partition that takes the rest. No separate
           recovery partition; WinRE lands inside the OS partition, which Setup
           accepts. DiskID 0 is the only disk in the domain — the CD-ROMs are
           not disks. -->
      <DiskConfiguration>
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order><Type>EFI</Type><Size>260</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order><Type>MSR</Type><Size>16</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order><Type>Primary</Type><Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order><PartitionID>1</PartitionID>
              <Format>FAT32</Format><Label>System</Label>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order><PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order><PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
              <Format>NTFS</Format><Label>Windows</Label><Letter>C</Letter>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/NAME</Key>
              <Value>@@IMAGENAME@@</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo>
            <DiskID>0</DiskID>
            <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
          </InstallTo>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <UserData>
        <!-- An empty Key is not an omission: it is what tells Setup there is no
             product key and to stop asking. -->
        <ProductKey>
          <Key/>
        </ProductKey>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
      </UserData>

    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="specialize">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <ComputerName>@@HOSTNAME@@</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>@@TIMEZONE@@</TimeZone>
    </component>

    <!-- Stage the post-Setup script. The helper ISO's letter is as unstable as
         any other, so the copy probes for a marker file instead of assuming. -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>create C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts</Description>
          <Path>cmd /c md C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Description>stage SetupComplete.cmd from the helper ISO</Description>
          <Path>cmd /c for %i in (D E F G H I) do @if exist %i:\vfioctl\setupcomplete.cmd copy /y %i:\vfioctl\setupcomplete.cmd C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\SetupComplete.cmd</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
      <InputLocale>@@INPUTLOCALE@@</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>@@SYSLOCALE@@</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>@@UILANG@@</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>@@USERLOCALE@@</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS"
               xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
               xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">

      <!-- A local account defined here is what keeps OOBE away from the network
           and the Microsoft-account screens; that path does not depend on the
           BypassNRO trick Microsoft removed in 24H2. -->
      <!-- PlainText, deliberately. Microsoft's alternative is base64 of
           UTF-16LE(password + the element's own name), which is reversible by
           anyone holding the file and therefore protects nothing; what it does
           add is a guess about which element name to append, and a wrong guess
           fails silently as an account whose password nobody knows. The
           rendered file lives 0600 in a 0700 directory and never leaves this
           machine — that is the actual protection. -->
      <UserAccounts>
        <LocalAccounts>
          <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
            <Name>@@USER@@</Name>
            <DisplayName>@@USER@@</DisplayName>
            <Group>Administrators</Group>
            <Password>
              <Value>@@PASSWORD@@</Value>
              <PlainText>true</PlainText>
            </Password>
          </LocalAccount>
        </LocalAccounts>
      </UserAccounts>

      <!-- THERE IS NO <AutoLogon> BLOCK HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN
           OVERSIGHT. The console session does have to be logged in for anything
           touching the display to work, because guest-exec runs in session 0
           where there are no display devices. But on 25H2 OOBE
           throws the answer file's autologon away:

             [CloudExperienceHostBroker.exe] Setting auto logon with
                 fUseAuthBuffer = 1, fEnableAutologon = 0
             [CloudExperienceHostBroker.exe] Clearing Auto-logon values per request

           (C:\Windows\Panther\UnattendGC\setupact.log, 2026-08-05.) A block
           that is silently discarded is worse than no block, because the next
           reader assumes it works. Autologon is set by the host afterwards over
           guest-exec instead — see guest/build.py. SetupComplete.cmd cannot
           do it either: it runs at 00:51:20 and OOBE clears the values at
           00:52:51, a minute and a half later. -->

      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
      </OOBE>

      <TimeZone>@@TIMEZONE@@</TimeZone>

    </component>
  </settings>

</unattend>
